e  1600s and early 1700s until we really  had a better understanding of what these  microbes actually were  pictured on the left here is anthony van  leeuwenhoek he was a famous draper and  nobleman in europe  and what he was really interested in  doing was figuring out the weaves of  different cloths  now as you know when you're weaving  cloth the more threads you have in a  small area the better the cloth and to  be able to assess how many threads there  were in a given let's say square inch of  cloth anthony van leeuwenhoek had been  playing around with trying to develop  better and better ways to magnify  the drapery  and what he ended up doing was actually  developing what we now know of as the  light microscope  now having this cool tool he decided to  not only look at drapery but also at  other interesting things around town and  so he ended up looking at pond scum he  looked at the gunk that was in between  his teeth remember that this was in an  era before common dental practices  and what he found amazed him he found  all kinds of different organisms big and  small rod shaped circular spiral  some were sedentary some moved  and he described these as tiny little  animals again or animal cules  pictured on the right are actual  drawings that lone hook draw drew of  these different bacteria that he was  seeing  and i would argue that this was actually  one of the first times anybody ever saw  a bacterium with their own eyes  of course over the subsequent years  especially in the 1800s people started  to be able to culture bacteria they  could get bacteria put them on these  petri dishes as you see on the left and  grow them up into numbers in the  millions and billions and even trillions  they then would take these bacteria put  them on a glass slide and stain them  with different stains and what you see  on the right side of this picture is a  gram stain which is a classic way to  describe bacteria some of you who are  scientifically oriented in the audience